//? Title: Generalized Linear Mixed Models: Working Fits and Partial Pooling
//? Subtitle: Hand calculations, PQL diagnostics, simulated counts and grouped penguin predictions with datamunge
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Binary outcomes and event counts often arrive in groups whose observations share
unmeasured influences. We want response probabilities or count means that account for
that dependence while sharing information across groups. A generalized linear mixed
model adds Gaussian group effects on a link scale. This report derives a working-model
PQL approximation, calculates two tiny updates by hand, profiles a four-count working
variance, and checks recovery on simulated groups with known effects. A penguin analysis
then separates species adjustment, prediction within observed islands and prediction
on an omitted island. Numerical convergence, working-Gaussian criteria and original
response likelihoods have different meanings throughout; setting a new group's effect
to zero also differs from averaging over its unknown effect. On the prespecified
100-penguin holdout, adding species increases accuracy from 81% to 89% and lowers
Brier loss from 0.1446 to 0.0744. The full-data island SD changes from 1.180 to a
zero-variance boundary after species adjustment.
)

\toc

> The method

>> Conditional observations and latent groups

For row \(i\) in group \(g\), a conditional GLMM specifies

$$
 b_g\sim N_q(0,D),\qquad
 \eta_i=x_i^\top\beta+z_i^\top b_g,\qquad
 E(Y_i\mid b_g)=\mu_i=g^{-1}(\eta_i).
$$
\alttext(Each group has a q dimensional Gaussian effect with covariance D. Its linear contribution adds to the fixed predictor before the inverse link gives the conditional response mean.)

`dm::stats::GLMM` supports Bernoulli zero/one observations with a logit link, and
nonnegative integer Poisson counts with a log link. Conditional dispersion is fixed at
one: \(V(\mu)=\mu(1-\mu)\) or \(\mu\). A random intercept multiplies a Poisson
mean by \(e^{b_g}\) and shifts binomial log odds by \(b_g\). These are link-scale
effects, not additive changes in counts or probabilities.

A formula such as `y ~ x + (1 + x | group)` uses fixed intercept/slope and a correlated
random intercept/slope. There is one grouping factor, and random slopes must be plain
numeric column names. This implementation has no trial-count weights, exposure offsets,
additional conditional dispersion, crossed groups or arbitrary links. Rows incomplete
for the fixed design, random design or group are omitted jointly; `kept_rows()` preserves
original indices. Retained numeric values must be finite. Null fixed predictors produce
NaN predictions; null required random slopes also produce NaN for a known group.

>> What the working fit approximates

An exact marginal likelihood would integrate the conditional density over every group
effect. For non-Gaussian observations, replacing that integral by a sequence of Gaussian
working fits is a statistical approximation, even if every working fit is solved accurately.
This differs from ordinary GLM IRLS, which can converge to the actual response-likelihood
maximum without making a Gaussian approximation to a latent-effect integral.

At the current conditional fit, linearizing the link gives a pseudo-response \(u\)
and diagonal working precision \(W\):

$$
 u_i=\eta_i+(y_i-\mu_i)g'(\mu_i),\qquad
 w_i=\frac1{V(\mu_i)g'(\mu_i)^2},\qquad
 u\approx X\beta+Zb+e,\quad e\sim N(0,W^{-1}).
$$
\alttext(The working response is the current predictor plus response error times the link derivative. The working precision combines the variance function and squared link derivative. A Gaussian mixed model is then fitted to these frozen pseudo-data.)

For logit, \(w_i=\mu_i(1-\mu_i)\); for Poisson log, \(w_i=\mu_i\).
The library initializes random effects at zero and, when there is a fixed intercept,
sets it to the link of the overall response mean. Training/working means are guarded
at \(10^{-8}\), and at \(1-10^{-8}\) for binomial. These guards prevent zero
working precision; they do not fix separation or certify a finite statistical optimum.

Writing \(D=\Lambda\Lambda^\top\), each group has working covariance
\(M_g=W_g^{-1}+Z_g D Z_g^\top\). Conditional on \(D\), Gaussian algebra gives

$$
 \widehat\beta=\left(\sum_gX_g^\top M_g^{-1}X_g\right)^{-1}
                 \sum_gX_g^\top M_g^{-1}u_g,\qquad
 \widehat b_g=D Z_g^\top M_g^{-1}(u_g-X_g\widehat\beta).
$$
\alttext(Profiled fixed effects solve generalized least squares across groups. Each group BLUP is its covariance times the random design transpose times the inverse-covariance-weighted working residual.)

For each frozen \(u,W\), this implementation estimates \(\Lambda\) by Gaussian
working ML, minimizing

$$
 Q(\Lambda;u,W)=n\log(2\pi)+\sum_g\log|M_g|
       +\sum_g(u_g-X_g\widehat\beta)^\top M_g^{-1}(u_g-X_g\widehat\beta).
$$
\alttext(The working objective sums Gaussian normalization, group covariance log determinants and the profiled residual quadratic. It is evaluated on the current frozen working responses and precisions.)

This is the implemented PQL variant: it uses profiled ML for working covariance, rather
than the default working REML in R's `MASS::glmmPQL`. Bounded differential evolution is
followed by a bounded local polish; the exact zero-variance candidate is also checked
for random-intercept models. A user can instead condition on a known random-intercept SD.
That option is used below to make the first updates hand-checkable.

`working_data(k)` reconstructs the inputs to update \(k+1\), starting at zero.
`iteration_history()` records completed updates. Its stopping flag requires changes
in fixed coefficients, group effects and covariance-factor entries to satisfy the
relative infinity-norm tolerance. A stationary fixed intercept alone is insufficient.
At the iteration limit, a finite last iterate is returned with `converged()` false.

>> Partial pooling and computation

With only a random intercept of variance \(\tau^2\), define
\(s_g=\sum_{i\in g}w_i\) and
\(\bar r_g=\sum_{i\in g}w_i(u_i-x_i^\top\widehat\beta)/s_g\).
The BLUP simplifies to

$$
 \widehat b_g=\frac{\tau^2s_g}{1+\tau^2s_g}\bar r_g.
$$
\alttext(The group effect equals its weighted working residual mean times a shrinkage factor between zero and one. More working information or larger group variance gives less shrinkage.)

This shows why the number of rows alone does not determine pooling: logit observations
near probabilities zero or one have little working information, whereas larger Poisson
means give larger log-scale information. When \(\tau=0\), group effects vanish and
the working fit reduces to ordinary GLM IRLS. Random-effect covariance boundaries deserve
attention, especially with very few groups; `covariance_at_boundary()` is a numerical
heuristic for singular-factor or search-box boundaries, not a variance-component test.

The implementation uses Woodbury identities with \(U_g=Z_g\Lambda\):

$$
 M_g^{-1}=W_g-W_gU_g(I+U_g^\top W_gU_g)^{-1}U_g^\top W_g,
 \qquad \log|M_g|=-\sum_{i\in g}\log w_i+\log|I+U_g^\top W_gU_g|.
$$
\alttext(A working covariance inverse and determinant are evaluated through a q by q system, avoiding a matrix factorization as large as the number of observations in a group.)

The formulas also work when \(D\) is singular because they never invert \(D\).
For \(n\) observations, \(G\) groups, \(p\) fixed and \(q\) random coordinates,
one objective evaluation costs \(O(n(p^2+pq+q^2)+Gq^3+p^3)\). Fitting multiplies
that work by all candidate evaluations across outer updates. Numerical normal equations
for fixed effects still limit accuracy with badly scaled or collinear designs.

>> Prediction targets and criteria

`predict()` uses a known group's fitted effect. An unseen, null or absent group falls
back to \(b=0\); `predict_fixed()` explicitly requests that zero-effect prediction.
It is not, in general, a new-group marginal mean. For a Poisson random intercept,

$$
 \mu_{b=0}(x)=e^{x^\top\widehat\beta},\qquad
 E_b[\mu(x,b)]=e^{x^\top\widehat\beta+\widehat\tau^2/2}.
$$
\alttext(Setting a Poisson group's effect to zero gives exp of the fixed predictor. Averaging over its Gaussian effect multiplies that value by exp of half the group variance.)

For logistic models the corresponding logistic-normal integral has no elementary closed
form, and the current prediction API does not evaluate it. No prediction intervals are
provided. Group BLUPs and their plug-in predictions condition on fitted variance components;
working-model fixed-effect Wald SEs do not propagate variance-component uncertainty.

`working_criterion()` is \(Q\) on the final pseudo-data. The historical `deviance()`
accessor returns that same quantity; historical `aic()` and `bic()` merely add parameter
penalties to it. They are not marginal GLMM likelihood criteria. In particular, values
of \(Q\) from different outer iterations or models use different pseudo-data and should
not rank original-response models. The report uses observed-response test losses instead.
PQL can be biased for binary data, especially with small clusters, and many rows in just
three groups do not provide a precise group-variance estimate.

> Simple examples

>> Four counts, two groups and a known variance

Take group A counts \((1,3)\), group B counts \((5,7)\), and fix \(\tau=1\).
Initially \(\beta=\log4\), \(b_A=b_B=0\), \(w_i=4\), and
\(u_i=\log4+(y_i-4)/4\). Both groups have \(s_g=8\), while their working
residual means are \(-1/2\) and \(+1/2\). Symmetry keeps the first fixed intercept
at \(\log4\), so

$$
 \widehat b_A^{(1)}=-\frac89\frac12=-\frac49,\qquad
 \widehat b_B^{(1)}=\frac49.
$$
\alttext(Each group's initial information is eight. With unit random-intercept variance the shrinkage factor is eight ninths, giving first group effects minus and plus four ninths.)

The figure shows the frozen working observations, their first fitted link values and the
subsequent conditional count means. The unchanged first fixed intercept must not stop
PQL: its group effects have moved substantially. The prediction table also contrasts
known-group conditional means, the zero-effect fallback for a new group, and the
Gaussian-averaged new-group mean. The latter ignores the query's group label and uses
the known variance one; it is a different target, not a prediction interval.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glmm_four_counts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{1,3,5,7});
  d.add_column("g",std::vector<std::string>{"A","A","B","B"});
  dm::stats::GLMMOptions opt;opt.family=dm::stats::GLMMFamily::Poisson;opt.fixed_random_intercept_sd=1;
  opt.max_iterations=1;const dm::stats::GLMM first(d,"y ~ 1 + (1|g)",opt);
  opt.max_iterations=50;const dm::stats::GLMM full(d,"y ~ 1 + (1|g)",opt);
  const auto w=first.working_data();auto working=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),fit=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  working.points(w.numeric_column("row").values(),w.numeric_column("z").values(),"working u");
  working.lines(std::vector<double>{0,1,2,3},first.linear_predictors(),"first eta",dm::RGB{220,38,38});
  working.title("First frozen Poisson working fit").x_label("row (zero-based)").y_label("link scale").y_limits(.5,2.7);
  fit.points(w.numeric_column("row").values(),d.numeric_column("y").values(),"observed");
  fit.lines(w.numeric_column("row").values(),first.fitted_values(),"first mean",dm::RGB{148,163,184});
  fit.lines(w.numeric_column("row").values(),full.fitted_values(),"final mean",dm::RGB{220,38,38});
  fit.title("Conditional count means").x_label("row (zero-based)").y_label("count or conditional mean").y_limits(0,9.5);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(working).add(fit).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glmm_four_counts.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame query;query.add_column("g",std::vector<std::string>{"A","B","new"});
  auto targets=full.prediction_frame(query);targets.add_column("group",std::vector<std::string>{"A","B","new"});
  targets.add_column("marginal_new_group",std::vector<double>(3,std::exp(full.coefficients()[0]+.5)));
  std::print("Prediction targets at the same fixed design:\n\n{}\n\n",targets.select({"group","group_status","fit","fixed_fit","marginal_new_group"}).to_md(6));
  std::print("Initial working data:\n\n{}\n\nFirst update:\n\n{}\n\nFinal group effects:\n\n{}\n\nStatus:\n\n{}\n",
      w.to_md(6),first.random_effects_frame().to_md(6),full.random_effects_frame().to_md(6),
      full.model_summary().select({"n","groups","iterations","converged"}).to_md());
}
```
