\caption(The first Poisson update has BLUPs exactly minus and plus four ninths. Continuing PQL relinearizes the response and weights; the final count means are not the raw group averages.)
\alttext(Initial working responses increase with the four counts. The first fitted predictors have one value in each group. On the response scale the final two group means pool toward the common centre.)

>> Eight binary responses and movement hidden by a fixed intercept

Use group A \((0,0,0,1)\) and B \((0,1,1,1)\), again with \(\tau=1\).
The overall proportion is one half: initial \(\beta=0\), \(w_i=1/4\), and
\(u_i=-2\) for a zero or \(+2\) for a one. Group working residual means are
\(-1,+1\), with \(s_g=1\), giving

$$
 \widehat\beta^{(1)}=0,\qquad
 \widehat b_A^{(1)}=-\tfrac12,\quad \widehat b_B^{(1)}=\tfrac12,
 \qquad \mu_A^{(1)}=\operatorname{logit}^{-1}(-1/2),\quad
 \mu_B^{(1)}=\operatorname{logit}^{-1}(1/2).
$$
\alttext(The first fixed intercept stays zero, but the two group effects become minus and plus one half. Their fitted probabilities are inverse-logit of those effects.)

The unpooled proportions are one quarter and three quarters. PQL with the known variance
shrinks the conditional probabilities toward one half. The first-step status is false
for convergence, correctly recording that the random effects changed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glmm_binary_groups.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,0,0,1,0,1,1,1});
  d.add_column("g",std::vector<std::string>{"A","A","A","A","B","B","B","B"});
  dm::stats::GLMMOptions opt;opt.fixed_random_intercept_sd=1;opt.max_iterations=1;
  const dm::stats::GLMM first(d,"y ~ 1 + (1|g)",opt);opt.max_iterations=50;
  const dm::stats::GLMM full(d,"y ~ 1 + (1|g)",opt);const auto h=full.iteration_history();
  auto means=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),path=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  means.points(std::vector<double>{0,1},std::vector<double>{.25,.75},"raw proportion",dm::RGB{148,163,184});
  means.points(std::vector<double>{0,1},std::vector<double>{first.fitted_values()[0],first.fitted_values()[4]},"first fit",dm::RGB{37,99,235});
  means.points(std::vector<double>{0,1},std::vector<double>{full.fitted_values()[0],full.fitted_values()[4]},"final fit",dm::RGB{220,38,38});
  means.abline_h(.5).title("Partial pooling of two binary groups").x_label("group A = 0, B = 1").y_label("conditional P(Y=1)").y_limits(.1,1.1);
  path.lines(h.numeric_column("iteration").values(),h.numeric_column("max_relative_change").values());
  path.title("Changes include the group effects").x_label("completed PQL update").y_label("maximum relative state change");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(means).add(path).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glmm_binary_groups.svg");
  std::print("First update:\n\n{}\n\nWorking observations:\n\n{}\n\nFinal groups:\n\n{}\n",
      first.model_summary().select({"n","iterations","converged"}).to_md(),first.working_data().to_md(4),full.random_effects_frame().to_md(6));
}
```
