> Checking the theory

>> A working-variance profile with an exact minimum

Return to the four counts and their first working state, now estimating \(\tau\).
Each group's working mean has residual variance \(1/8\), and the two means lie
\(1/2\) below or above \(\log4\). After profiling the common mean, the part of
\(Q\) that depends on \(t=\tau^2\) is

$$
 Q(t)=C+2\log(t+1/8)+\frac{1/2}{t+1/8},\qquad
 Q'(t)=\frac2{t+1/8}-\frac{1/2}{(t+1/8)^2},\qquad
 \widehat t=\frac18.
$$
\alttext(The variance-dependent working criterion is twice log t plus one eighth, plus one half divided by t plus one eighth. Its derivative vanishes at t equals one eighth.)

Thus the first estimated SD is \(\sqrt{1/8}\), the shrinkage factor is one half,
and the first effects are \(-1/4,+1/4\). This is an ML result for a *single frozen
Gaussian working dataset*, not an exact Poisson GLMM variance estimate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glmm_working_profile.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{1,3,5,7});
  d.add_column("g",std::vector<std::string>{"A","A","B","B"});
  dm::stats::GLMMOptions opt;opt.family=dm::stats::GLMMFamily::Poisson;opt.max_iterations=1;
  const dm::stats::GLMM m(d,"y ~ 1 + (1|g)",opt);
  const auto tau=dm::seql(0,1.2,121);std::vector<double> criterion,shrink;
  for(double t:tau.values()){criterion.push_back(m.working_criterion_at({t},0));shrink.push_back(8*t*t/(1+8*t*t));}
  auto profile=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),pool=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  profile.lines(tau.values(),criterion).abline_v(std::sqrt(.125)).title("One frozen working-ML profile").x_label("random-intercept SD tau").y_label("working criterion Q");
  pool.lines(tau.values(),shrink).abline_v(std::sqrt(.125)).abline_h(.5).title("Corresponding shrinkage factor").x_label("random-intercept SD tau").y_label("fraction of raw working effect");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(profile).add(pool).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glmm_working_profile.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame result;result.add_column("estimated_variance",std::vector<double>{m.random_effect_covariance()(0,0)});
  result.add_column("exact_variance",std::vector<double>{.125});result.add_column("first_A_effect",std::vector<double>{m.random_effects()[0][0]});
  std::print("{}\n",result.to_md(8));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| estimated_variance | exact_variance | first_A_effect |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.12500000 | 0.125 | -0.25000000 |

\image(figures/glmm_working_profile.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The exact first-working-fit variance optimum is one eighth. Its shrinkage factor is one half; the vertical line marks the corresponding SD.)
\alttext(A working-criterion curve has its minimum at SD square root of one eighth. The shrinkage curve rises with SD and crosses one half at that same point.)

>> More information within simulated groups

The reusable generator makes 25 groups with Gaussian log-scale effects of SD 0.5,
conditional intercept 1 and slope 0.4. Within each group, \(x\) spans minus one to
one, and observations are Poisson. It draws all group effects before any responses,
so the same seed retains exactly the same effects across cluster-size experiments.
`true_effect` and `true_mean` are evaluation columns, never model predictors.

We fit 6, 18 and 54 observations per group. The table compares recovered coefficients,
estimated SD and group-effect RMSE against known truth. One realization can fluctuate;
these are controlled examples of information and recovery, not a Monte Carlo proof
of unbiasedness or a guarantee that every larger sample has smaller realized error.
There are still only 25 independent group-effect draws: increasing rows within these
groups cannot force their realized spread to equal the generating population SD.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glmm_simulated_counts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::vector<double> size,intercept,slope,sd,error;std::vector<std::string> converged;
  auto recovery=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for(std::size_t count:{6,18,54}) {
    const auto d=dm::stats::simulate_glmm_random_intercepts(dm::stats::GLMMFamily::Poisson,25,count,1,.4,.5,42);
    dm::stats::GLMMOptions opt;opt.family=dm::stats::GLMMFamily::Poisson;opt.max_iterations=60;
    const dm::stats::GLMM m(d,"y ~ x + (1|group)",opt);
    std::vector<double> truth,estimate;double squared=0;
    for(std::size_t g=0;g<m.num_groups();++g){truth.push_back(d.double_at("true_effect",g*count));estimate.push_back(m.random_effects()[g][0]);squared+=std::pow(estimate.back()-truth.back(),2);}
    size.push_back(count);intercept.push_back(m.coefficients()[0]);slope.push_back(m.coefficients()[1]);sd.push_back(m.random_effect_std_devs()[0]);
    error.push_back(std::sqrt(squared/m.num_groups()));converged.push_back(m.converged()?"yes":"no");
    if(count==18) recovery.points(truth,estimate,"25 groups");
  }
  recovery.abline(0,1).title("Known effects versus fitted BLUPs").x_label("true group log effect").y_label("estimated group log effect").x_limits(-1.3,1.3).y_limits(-1.3,1.8);
  auto information=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();information.lines(size,error,"effect RMSE",dm::RGB{220,38,38});
  information.title("Realized group-effect recovery").x_label("observations per group").y_label("log-scale group-effect RMSE");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(recovery).add(information).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glmm_simulated_counts.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame out;out.add_column("rows_per_group",size);out.add_column("intercept_hat",intercept);out.add_column("slope_hat",slope);
  out.add_column("group_SD_hat",sd);out.add_column("effect_RMSE",error);out.add_column("converged",converged);std::print("{}\n",out.to_md(6));
}
```
