// result_end

\caption(Left: group BLUPs versus the actual generating effects for 18 observations per group, with the identity line. Right: realized group-effect RMSE across the three cluster sizes.)
\alttext(Twenty-five estimated group effects scatter around their known values. A second panel and table compare effect-recovery errors and parameter estimates as observations per group increase.)

> Penguins: a grouped binary response

>> Data, units and group limitations

`dm::datasets::penguins()` contains 344 penguins, with species, island, sex and four
morphological measurements. Sex is missing for 11 rows. We omit incomplete sex/body-mass
rows, encode male as one and use centred body mass in kilograms,
`mass = body_mass_g/1000 - 4.2`. The binary response is natural; no thresholded or rounded
measurement is invented to obtain it. The intercept refers to a 4.2 kg penguin.

Only three islands are available. They are a small, geographically specific set, rather
than a large random sample from an island population. Gentoo occurs on Biscoe only,
Chinstrap on Dream only, while Adelie occurs on all three. Island effects from a model
that omits species can therefore absorb species composition. We first summarize that
composition, then compare the descriptive mass-only model to a species-adjusted fit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::penguins().filter([](const auto& r){return !r.is_null("sex") && !r.is_null("body_mass_g");})
      .mutate_with("male",[](const auto& r){return r.get_string("sex")=="male"?1.0:0.0;});
  std::print("{}\n",d | dm::group_by("island","species") | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("male","male_fraction"),dm::agg::mean("body_mass_g","mean_mass_g")) | dm::to_md(4));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| island | species | n | male_fraction | mean_mass_g |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Torgersen | Adelie | 47 | 0.4894 | 3708.5106 |
| Biscoe | Adelie | 44 | 0.5000 | 3709.6591 |
| Dream | Adelie | 55 | 0.5091 | 3701.3636 |
| Biscoe | Gentoo | 119 | 0.5126 | 5092.4370 |
| Dream | Chinstrap | 68 | 0.5000 | 3733.0882 |
// result_end

\caption(Complete-case island/species composition and sex proportions. The grouping structure and species imbalance constrain what an island variance can mean.)

>> Species adjustment changes the apparent island variation

Both models below use the same 333 complete rows. They are descriptive fits, with
working-model SEs conditional on the fitted covariance. Their working criteria are not
used as marginal-likelihood AIC scores. Curves hold species at Adelie, the species present
on all islands, rather than extrapolating Gentoo to islands where it was not observed.
A zero estimated variance is a boundary solution, not evidence that every possible island
population has exactly zero heterogeneity.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glmm_penguins_islands.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::penguins().filter([](const auto& r){return !r.is_null("sex") && !r.is_null("body_mass_g");})
      .mutate_with("male",[](const auto& r){return r.get_string("sex")=="male"?1.0:0.0;})
      .mutate_with("mass",[](const auto& r){return r.get_double("body_mass_g")/1000-4.2;});
  dm::graphics::RLayout layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);dm::dstruct::DataFrame status;
  for(int adjusted:{0,1}) {
    dm::stats::GLMMOptions opt;opt.max_iterations=60;
    const dm::stats::GLMM m(d,adjusted?"male ~ mass + species + (1|island)":"male ~ mass + (1|island)",opt);
    const std::string label=adjusted?"species adjusted":"mass only";
    auto row=m.model_summary().select({"n","groups","iterations","converged","covariance_boundary"});
    row.add_column("model",std::vector<std::string>{label});row.add_column("island_SD",std::vector<double>{m.random_effect_std_devs()[0]});
    status=adjusted?status.concat_rows(row):row;
    auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();const auto mass=dm::seql(-1.5,.8,151);
    const std::vector<dm::RGB> colors={{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{22,163,74}};
    for(std::size_t g=0;g<m.num_groups();++g){dm::dstruct::DataFrame q;q.add_column("mass",mass.values());
      q.add_column("island",std::vector<std::string>(151,m.group_labels()[g]));q.add_column("species",std::vector<std::string>(151,"Adelie"));
      plot.lines((mass+4.2).values(),m.predict(q),m.group_labels()[g],colors[g]);}
    plot.title(label+": conditional island curves").x_label("body mass (kg); Adelie if adjusted").y_label("conditional P(male)").y_limits(-.03,1.4);
    layout.add(plot);std::print("{} coefficients:\n\n{}\n\n{} group effects:\n\n{}\n\n",label,m.coefficient_frame().to_md(5),label,m.random_effects_frame().to_md(5));
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1100,500).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glmm_penguins_islands.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",status.select({"model","n","groups","island_SD","iterations","converged","covariance_boundary"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
mass only coefficients:

| term | estimate | std_error | z_value | p_value |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| (Intercept) | 0.34171 | 0.69722 | 0.49011 | 0.62406 |
| mass | 2.45169 | 0.29418 | 8.33388 | 0.00000 |


mass only group effects:

| group | n | (Intercept) |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| Torgersen | 47 | 0.78012 |
| Biscoe | 163 | -1.63199 |
| Dream | 123 | 0.85188 |


species adjusted coefficients:

| term | estimate | std_error | z_value | p_value |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| (Intercept) | 3.83408 | 0.50559 | 7.58334 | 0.00000 |
| mass | 7.37284 | 0.81415 | 9.05591 | 0.00000 |
| speciesChinstrap | -0.25593 | 0.42931 | -0.59613 | 0.55109 |
| speciesGentoo | -10.17781 | 1.19460 | -8.51982 | 0.00000 |


species adjusted group effects:

| group | n | (Intercept) |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| Torgersen | 47 | 0 |
| Biscoe | 163 | 0 |
| Dream | 123 | 0 |


| model | n | groups | island_SD | iterations | converged | covariance_boundary |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |
| mass only | 333 | 3 | 1.179775 | 6 | yes | no |
| species adjusted | 333 | 3 | 0.000000 | 7 | yes | yes |

\image(figures/glmm_penguins_islands.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Descriptive mass-only and species-adjusted fits, including island BLUPs, working SEs and numerical status. Conditional curves are shown for each island; adjusted curves use Adelie throughout.)
\alttext(Two panels compare island-specific fitted sex probabilities before and after species adjustment. Tables expose the fixed coefficients, island effects and any zero-variance boundary.)

The mass-only fit estimates island SD 1.1798, while the species-adjusted fit reaches
exactly zero and sets every island BLUP to zero. Its three Adelie curves coincide for
that reason. This is consistent with species composition accounting for much of the
apparent island variation in this specification; it does not identify a causal island
effect or validate an island population variance from three locations.

>> A prespecified holdout within the observed islands

We split once with `train_test_split(...,0.3,42,"island")`. Island stratification retains
training observations from each island, so these test predictions may legitimately use
island effects learned from other penguins. Test labels never enter those effects.
Two formulas are prespecified: mass only, and mass plus species. Brier loss averages
\((\widehat p-y)^2\); log loss averages negative Bernoulli log probabilities. Lower is
better for both. Accuracy uses the fixed threshold one half. A small single split is
an illustration, not a calibrated uncertainty assessment or a model-selection guarantee.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glmm_penguins_holdout.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::penguins().filter([](const auto& r){return !r.is_null("sex") && !r.is_null("body_mass_g");})
      .mutate_with("male",[](const auto& r){return r.get_string("sex")=="male"?1.0:0.0;})
      .mutate_with("mass",[](const auto& r){return r.get_double("body_mass_g")/1000-4.2;});
  const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(d,.3,42,"island");dm::dstruct::DataFrame scores;
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  for(int adjusted:{0,1}){
    dm::stats::GLMMOptions opt;opt.max_iterations=60;
    const dm::stats::GLMM m(train,adjusted?"male ~ mass + species + (1|island)":"male ~ mass + (1|island)",opt);
    const std::string label=adjusted?"species adjusted":"mass only";
    auto row=m.binomial_score(test);row.add_column("model",std::vector<std::string>{label});
    row.add_column("converged",std::vector<std::string>{m.converged()?"yes":"no"});scores=adjusted?scores.concat_rows(row):row;
    auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for(const auto& sex:{"female","male"}){const auto group=test.filter([&](const auto&r){return r.get_string("sex")==sex;});
      plot.points((dm::Vector<>(group.numeric_column("mass").values())+4.2).values(),m.predict(group),sex,
          std::string(sex)=="female"?dm::RGB{37,99,235}:dm::RGB{220,38,38});}
    plot.abline_h(.5).title(label+": held-out predictions").x_label("body mass (kg)").y_label("conditional P(male)").y_limits(-.03,1.3);layout.add(plot);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/glmm_penguins_holdout.svg");
  std::print("Training rows: {}; test rows: {}.\n\n{}\n",train.nrows(),test.nrows(),scores.select({"model","n","accuracy","brier","log_loss","converged"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Training rows: 233; test rows: 100.

| model | n | accuracy | brier | log_loss | converged |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| mass only | 100 | 0.81 | 0.144562 | 0.453253 | yes |
| species adjusted | 100 | 0.89 | 0.074401 | 0.244815 | yes |

\image(figures/glmm_penguins_holdout.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Observed-island holdout predictions and probability losses. Every test island is represented in training. Points are coloured by observed sex; the horizontal line is the classification threshold.)
\alttext(Two panels compare test probabilities from mass-only and species-adjusted mixed models, with observed male and female labels and a one-half threshold. The table gives row counts, accuracy and probability losses.)

On this split, the adjusted fit correctly classifies 89 of 100 test penguins versus
81 for mass only; Brier loss falls from 0.1446 to 0.0744 and log loss from 0.4533 to
0.2448. The two prespecified candidates were not tuned on these test labels.

>> Omit a whole island: a different prediction task

Now fit on two islands and assess the third, using the mass-only formula in every fold.
Species adjustment is deliberately excluded here: omitting Biscoe or Dream would also
remove an entire species from training, so its fixed effect could not be estimated.
The omitted island is unseen. `prediction_frame()` therefore uses the zero-random-effect
baseline, not a BLUP learned from its test labels and not a logistic-normal marginal mean.
These three folds are too few to estimate a reliable distribution of performance over
new islands. Their species mixes and covariate distributions also differ, so scores should
be interpreted fold by fold rather than as a direct ranking against the within-island split.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/glmm_penguins_new_island.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::penguins().filter([](const auto& r){return !r.is_null("sex") && !r.is_null("body_mass_g");})
      .mutate_with("male",[](const auto& r){return r.get_string("sex")=="male"?1.0:0.0;})
      .mutate_with("mass",[](const auto& r){return r.get_double("body_mass_g")/1000-4.2;});
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame scores;std::vector<double> index,brier;
  const std::vector<std::string> islands={"Torgersen","Biscoe","Dream"};
  for(std::size_t g=0;g<islands.size();++g){
    const auto train=d.filter([&](const auto&r){return r.get_string("island")!=islands[g];});
    const auto test=d.filter([&](const auto&r){return r.get_string("island")==islands[g];});
    dm::stats::GLMMOptions opt;opt.max_iterations=60;
    const dm::stats::GLMM m(train,"male ~ mass + (1|island)",opt);auto row=m.binomial_score(test);
    row.add_column("omitted_island",std::vector<std::string>{islands[g]});row.add_column("prediction",std::vector<std::string>{m.prediction_frame(test).string_at("group_status",0)});
    row.add_column("converged",std::vector<std::string>{m.converged()?"yes":"no"});scores=g?scores.concat_rows(row):row;
    index.push_back(g);brier.push_back(row.double_at("brier",0));
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.points(index,brier,"Brier loss",dm::RGB{37,99,235});
  plot.title("Zero-effect predictions on an omitted island").x_label("0 = Torgersen, 1 = Biscoe, 2 = Dream").y_label("Brier loss").y_limits(0,.5);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");plot.size(850,490).save_svg("build/glmm_penguins_new_island.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",scores.select({"omitted_island","n","prediction","accuracy","brier","log_loss","converged"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| omitted_island | n | prediction | accuracy | brier | log_loss | converged |
| :--- | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| Torgersen | 47 | unseen | 0.659574 | 0.199408 | 0.573055 | yes |
| Biscoe | 163 | unseen | 0.588957 | 0.381249 | 2.296045 | yes |
| Dream | 123 | unseen | 0.634146 | 0.218527 | 0.619016 | yes |

\image(figures/glmm_penguins_new_island.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Whole-island holdouts use the zero-effect fallback for an unseen group. Reported probability scores concern that explicit prediction rule, not integration over a new island effect.)
\alttext(Three points give separate Brier losses for Torgersen, Biscoe and Dream when each island is withheld from fitting. The table reports every fold as an unseen-group prediction task.)

> Conclusion

The tiny examples expose partial pooling and demonstrate that fixed coefficients can stop
moving before group effects do. The first frozen Poisson variance profile has an exact
minimum, while simulation checks recovery against actual generating effects. On penguins,
island variation is sensitive to species composition and is supported by only three groups.
Predictions within observed islands and on omitted islands use different information.
PQL remains an approximation, its working criteria are not marginal GLMM likelihoods,
and a zero-effect prediction is a conditional baseline rather than a population average.

> References

- Breslow, N. E. and Clayton, D. G. (1993). [Approximate Inference in Generalized Linear Mixed Models|https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1993.10594284]. ~Journal of the American Statistical Association~, 88(421), 9–25. PQL, variance estimation and approximation limitations.
- R MASS documentation. [Fit Generalized Linear Mixed Models via PQL|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/MASS/html/glmmPQL.html]. Working mixed-model fitting; the present implementation uses working ML.
- Gorman, K. B., Williams, T. D. and Fraser, W. R. (2014). [Ecological Sexual Dimorphism and Environmental Variability within a Community of Antarctic Penguins (genus Pygoscelis)|https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0090081]. ~PLOS ONE~, 9(3), e90081. Source of the Palmer Archipelago measurements, used through `dm::datasets::penguins()`.
- Horst, A. M., Hill, A. P. and Gorman, K. B. [palmerpenguins|https://allisonhorst.github.io/palmerpenguins/]. Dataset structure and species/island context.

